  "worked_solution": "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(4+3\\text{i}) \\\\ (-1)(4+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4+3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}.$",
